  "costUsd": null,
  "content": "Putting is where the team is losing the most strokes, and it's not close. Every one of the seven active players is in the red there, averaging about **-3.0 strokes per round** across recent windows of 11-18 rounds each. Approach play is the next largest leak (team average roughly **-2.35 strokes/round**), also negative for all seven. Off the tee, by contrast, five of seven players are gaining strokes, and around-the-green play is close to neutral (team average about -0.15).\n\nBy player, strokes gained putting over their most recent tracked rounds:\n\n| Player | SG Putting | Rounds |\n|---|---|---|\n| Owen Carter | -2.16 | 12 |\n| Ethan Park | -2.72 | 13 |\n| Mason Rivers | -3.00 | 12 |\n| Dylan Brooks | -3.00 | 12 |\n| Tyler Hayes | -3.34 | 11 |\n| Jackson Hale | -3.91 | 15 |\n| Cole Bennett | -3.92 | 18 |\n\nCole Bennett and Jackson Hale show the largest putting deficits, but the whole roster is underwater on the green — this reads as a team-wide issue rather than one or two players. Given that, a team putting focus (or a shared practice block on speed/lag putting) would likely move the needle more than individual fixes.\n\n_Some figures in this answer could not be traced back to your program's data, so I've flagged it rather than presenting them as fact. Please ask again._",
